{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Hodge diamonds · {% endblock %}
{% block head %}
<script defer src="{{ root }}assets/hodge.js"></script>
{% endblock %}
{% block main %}
<header class="page-heading">
  <p class="kicker">Geometry</p>
  <h1>Hodge diamonds</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The Hodge numbers \(h^{p,q}\) of a smooth complex projective variety of
    complex dimension \(n\), arranged by total degree \(k = p + q\). This page
    is a teaching widget, not catalogue data: it draws the diamond, draws the
    lines of its symmetries, and names what a click selects. The symmetries it
    draws are exactly the ones the catalogue checks on every geometric card —
    conjugation \(h^{p,q} = h^{q,p}\) and Serre duality
    \(h^{p,q} = h^{n-p,n-q}\) — plus the square mirror
    \(h^{p,q} = h^{n-p,q}\) whose presence the catalogue records as
    <code>symmetry_group: D4</code> (see
    <a href="https://hyperkaehler.info/hodge/">hyperkaehler.info/hodge</a>).
  </p>
</header>

<section aria-labelledby="controls">
  <h2 id="controls">The diamond</h2>
  <form class="hodge-controls" onsubmit="return false">
    <label for="hodge-dim">Complex dimension \(n\) — sets the size of the diamond</label>
    <div class="hodge-row">
      <input id="hodge-dim" type="range" min="0" max="6" step="1" value="2">
      <output id="hodge-dim-value" for="hodge-dim">2</output>
    </div>
    <label for="hodge-preset">Preset</label>
    <select id="hodge-preset">
      <option value="general">General symbols \(h^{p,q}\)</option>
      <option value="projective">Projective space \(\mathbf{P}^n\)</option>
      <option value="elliptic">Elliptic curve</option>
      <option value="k3" selected>K3 surface{% if has_k3 %} (numbers of the k3-surface card, linked below){% endif %}</option>
      <option value="cy3">Calabi–Yau threefold, \(h^{1,1} = a\), \(h^{2,1} = b\)</option>
      <option value="hyperkahler">Hyperkähler (K3 numbers, D4 mirrors on)</option>
    </select>
    <div class="hodge-row" id="hodge-ab-row" hidden>
      <label for="hodge-a">\(a = h^{1,1}\)</label>
      <input id="hodge-a" type="range" min="0" max="20" step="1" value="2">
      <output id="hodge-a-value" for="hodge-a">2</output>
      <label for="hodge-b">\(b = h^{2,1}\)</label>
      <input id="hodge-b" type="range" min="0" max="120" step="1" value="1">
      <output id="hodge-b-value" for="hodge-b">1</output>
    </div>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Symmetry lines to draw (the orbit of a click is the group they generate)</legend>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="hodge-sym-conj" checked> Conjugation, vertical line</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="hodge-sym-serre" checked> Serre duality, half turn about the centre</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="hodge-sym-horiz"> Conjugation then Serre, horizontal line</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="hodge-sym-diag"> Square mirror (the D4 extra)</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="hodge-sym-rot"> Quarter turn (the D4 extra)</label>
    </fieldset>
  </form>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <svg id="hodge-svg" role="img" aria-label="Hodge diamond"></svg>
  </div>
  <div id="hodge-info" hidden>
    <p id="hodge-orbit"></p>
    <p id="hodge-filtration"></p>
    <p id="hodge-totals"></p>
  </div>
  <noscript><p>This page needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="symmetries">
  <h2 id="symmetries">The symmetries</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Three of the five toggles hold on every smooth complex projective
      variety, and the catalogue checks two of them on every card. Conjugation
      sends \((p, q)\) to \((q, p)\), the vertical line. Serre duality sends
      \((p, q)\) to \((n-p, n-q)\), the half turn about the centre. Their
      composite sends \((p, q)\) to \((n-q, n-p)\), the horizontal line.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other two hold exactly where the catalogue records
      <code>symmetry_group: D4</code>, the hyperkähler case. The square mirror
      sends \((p, q)\) to \((n-p, q)\), the diagonal line; with the three above
      it generates the eight symmetries of the square. The quarter turn sends
      \((p, q)\) to \((n-q, p); it is the square mirror followed by Serre
      duality. Toggling these two on another preset draws identifications that
      do not hold there; they are for inspecting the hyperkähler diamond.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="reading">
  <h2 id="reading">How to read it</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Row \(k\) of the diamond, from the top, is the cohomology
      \(H^k = \bigoplus_{p+q=k} H^{p,q}\).
      Clicking the diamond for \(h^{p,q}\) names its orbit under the checked
      symmetries, and highlights the piece \(F^p H^k\) of the Hodge filtration
      that the click selects:
      \(F^p H^k = \bigoplus_{i \geq p} H^{i,k-i}\), the diamonds of row \(k\)
      from column \(p\) rightwards. Its rank is the sum shown when the preset
      has numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The projective-space values are the standard computation of the
      cohomology of \(\mathbf{P}^n\)
      (<a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FMG">Stacks Project,
      0FMG</a>); the K3 values are {% if has_k3 %}the ones of the
      <a href="{{ root }}geometric-objects/k3-surface.html">k3-surface</a>
      card{% else %}the standard K3 Hodge numbers{% endif %}.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
